//! lotus/stereo_shift.rye -- ALES124: stereo_shift -- the grid nudge carried into stereo, both channels in lockstep. ALES108 named `shift` -- slide the whole clip by `by` samples and DROP
//! what falls off the edge, filling the vacated end with silence, so the length HOLDS -- for a mono Clip. Yet a Lotus master is a StereoClip (ALES10), two Clips heard together whose defining
//! invariant is that LEFT and RIGHT hold the SAME length so the two channels stay aligned in time. This rung carries shift into stereo the way ALES122 carried silence_span: run ALES108's
//! proven mono `shift` on each channel with the SAME signed `by`, so nudging a take earlier or later on the grid slides both speakers by the same amount at once. It is the plainest lift of
//! the family -- mono shift is TOTAL (it names no out-of-range span, so it raises no fault), so this stereo twin needs no validation and can never refuse, and the length-preserving reseat
//! beside stereo_silence_span (which overwrites in place) and stereo_move (which relocates).
//!
//! The rule, stated once: stereo_shift(sc, by) slides both channels by the same signed `by`, reusing ALES108's mono `shift` per channel over its public API. Because mono shift is total and
//! holds each channel's length, the balance invariant is preserved trivially -- both channels enter equal-length, both slide by the same magnitude, both hold their length, so they leave
//! equal-length; there is no fault to forward, no EditError, no `try`. Negative `by` slides LEFT (earlier), positive slides RIGHT (later); a shift at or past the length leaves an all-silent
//! channel. One signed `by` for both channels, because a stereo master is nudged on the grid as one thing.
//!
//! The crux -- four laws. THE STEREO SHIFT LAW: the left channel equals mono shift(left, by) and the right equals mono shift(right, by), each byte for byte in both directions, the surviving
//! samples sliding by `by` and the vacated end silent. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW: left.len == right.len after the edit and both hold their starting length -- shift reseats values and fills
//! silence, never resizing, so the stereo image stays aligned. THE COMPOSITION-LOSS LAW, HONEST: stereo_shift(k) then stereo_shift(-k) returns the leading k samples as SILENCE on both
//! channels, not their bytes -- shift is not invertible, the dropped edge is gone; only a stereo clip whose shifted-off region was already silence round-trips. THE MAGNITUDE / DEGENERATE LAW:
//! the peak magnitude of each channel (re-measured through ALES13) is non-increasing; by 0 the identity on both, |by| >= len all silence on both, the empty stereo pair its own shift.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES124.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-023038_fill-ales124-lotus-stereo-shift.md
//! Stands on: lotus/shift.rye (ALES108) -- the mono grid nudge run once per channel; lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) -- the StereoClip (two mono Clips, left/right) and its balance invariant;
//! lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) -- the Clip and its buf/len/samples; lotus/meter.rye (ALES13) -- the peak/RMS meter, used by the witness to prove dropping an edge never raises a channel's peak.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE -- software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) on one bench, siloed to lotus/. It reseats existing samples in each channel and fills the vacated
//! end with SILENCE (zero) -- the one thing it writes that was not already there, and silence is the honest content of an edge a clip was nudged away from; it invents no non-zero sample,
//! changes no length, and reads no byte past either channel's len. No real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_shift.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const shift = @import("shift.rye");
const meter = @import("meter.rye");

pub const stereo_shift_version = "20260815.023038";

/// Slide both channels of `sc` by the same signed `by` samples in lockstep, dropping what falls off each edge and filling each vacated end with silence, the stereo image staying balanced
/// and each channel's length holding. Negative `by` shifts left (earlier), positive shifts right (later). Total on any balanced StereoClip: mono shift raises no fault and holds each length,
/// so the two channels leave equal-length and no refusal can land between them. Reuses ALES108's mono shift per channel.
pub fn stereo_shift(sc: *pan.StereoClip, by: i32) void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced -- the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the edit preserves.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);

    // Mono shift is total and holds the length, so each channel slides the same magnitude and no fault can land between the two edits.
    shift.shift(&sc.left, by);
    shift.shift(&sc.right, by);

    // invariant: the channels leave balanced -- each held its length under the same shift, so the stereo image stays aligned.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE STEREO SHIFT LAW -- each channel equals ALES108's mono shift on that channel byte for byte, both directions, the vacated end silent; THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW -- left.len ==
//     right.len after, both holding their starting length; THE COMPOSITION-LOSS LAW -- stereo_shift(k) then stereo_shift(-k) zeros the leading k on both, a pre-silent region round-tripping;
//     THE MAGNITUDE / DEGENERATE LAW -- each channel's peak non-increasing, by 0 the identity, |by| >= len all silence, the empty pair its own shift ----------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

/// A channel's current peak magnitude, re-measured through ALES13 -- the reader the magnitude law checks each channel against.
fn clip_peak(clip: *const timeline.Clip) !u32 {
    const m = try meter.measure(clip);
    return meter.peak(&m);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. THE STEREO SHIFT LAW -- each channel equals ALES108's mono shift on that channel, proven side by side over one anchor with different per-channel samples, both directions.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 }, &[_]i16{ 11, 21, 31, 41, 51 });
        stereo_shift(&sc, 2); // right by 2 in both channels

        var ml = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&ml, 0, &[_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 });
        shift.shift(&ml, 2);
        var mr = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mr, 0, &[_]i16{ 11, 21, 31, 41, 51 });
        shift.shift(&mr, 2);

        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), ml.samples())) return error.LeftChannelWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), mr.samples())) return error.RightChannelWrong;
        // Concretely: {0,0,10,20,30} on the left, {0,0,11,21,31} on the right; the last two dropped, two zeros in front.
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 10, 20, 30 })) return error.LeftShiftWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 11, 21, 31 })) return error.RightShiftWrong;

        // The left direction lands the mirror image on both channels.
        var lft = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&lft, &[_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 }, &[_]i16{ 11, 21, 31, 41, 51 });
        stereo_shift(&lft, -2); // left by 2 in both channels
        if (!eq(lft.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 30, 40, 50, 0, 0 })) return error.LeftDirLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(lft.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 31, 41, 51, 0, 0 })) return error.LeftDirRightWrong;
    }

    // 2. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW -- the channels stay equal length and each holds its starting length; the stereo image stays aligned. Proven both directions.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 }, &[_]i16{ 6, 7, 8 });
        stereo_shift(&sc, 1);
        if (sc.left.len != sc.right.len) return error.Unbalanced;
        if (sc.left.len != 3) return error.LengthMoved;
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 0, 1, 2 })) return error.BalanceLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 0, 6, 7 })) return error.BalanceRightWrong;
    }

    // 3. THE COMPOSITION-LOSS LAW -- stereo_shift is NOT invertible: shift(k) then shift(-k) returns the leading k samples as silence on both channels, the dropped edge gone; yet a
    //    pre-silent region round-trips exactly, because nothing non-zero is lost.
    {
        var lossy = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&lossy, &[_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 }, &[_]i16{ 11, 21, 31, 41, 51 });
        stereo_shift(&lossy, 2); // {0,0,10,20,30} | {0,0,11,21,31}
        stereo_shift(&lossy, -2); // 40,50 (and 41,51) are gone — the leading survivors return, the tail is silence
        if (!eq(lossy.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 0, 0 })) return error.LossyLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(lossy.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 11, 21, 31, 0, 0 })) return error.LossyRightWrong;

        var presilent = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&presilent, &[_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 0, 0 }, &[_]i16{ 11, 21, 31, 0, 0 });
        stereo_shift(&presilent, -2); // the two trailing zeros dropped cost nothing on both channels
        stereo_shift(&presilent, 2);
        if (!eq(presilent.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 30, 0, 0 })) return error.PreSilentLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(presilent.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 31, 0, 0 })) return error.PreSilentRightWrong;
    }

    // 4a. THE MAGNITUDE LAW -- each channel's peak re-measured through ALES13 is non-increasing: a shift that drops a loud edge strictly lowers that channel's peak, a shift that drops only
    //     silence holds it.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ -30000, 100, -200, 300 }, &[_]i16{ 50, 60, 70, 0 });
        const lb = try clip_peak(&sc.left);
        const rb = try clip_peak(&sc.right);
        stereo_shift(&sc, -1); // drops the loud -30000 off the left front; the right drops only 50 (its quiet head) yet keeps its own peak 70
        const la = try clip_peak(&sc.left);
        const ra = try clip_peak(&sc.right);
        if (!(la <= lb)) return error.LeftPeakRose;
        if (!(la < lb)) return error.LeftPeakShouldHaveFallen;
        if (!(ra <= rb)) return error.RightPeakRose;
    }

    // 4b. THE DEGENERATE LAW -- by 0 the identity on both channels, |by| >= len all silence on both, the empty stereo pair its own shift, balanced throughout.
    {
        var idc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&idc, &[_]i16{ 7, 8, 9 }, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 });
        stereo_shift(&idc, 0);
        if (!eq(idc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 7, 8, 9 })) return error.ZeroLeftMoved;
        if (!eq(idc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 })) return error.ZeroRightMoved;

        var out = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&out, &[_]i16{ 7, 8, 9 }, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 });
        stereo_shift(&out, 9); // far past len — clamped, both channels all silence
        if (!eq(out.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0 })) return error.OutLeftNotSilenced;
        if (!eq(out.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0 })) return error.OutRightNotSilenced;
        if (out.left.len != out.right.len) return error.OutUnbalanced;

        var empty = pan.StereoClip{};
        stereo_shift(&empty, 5);
        if (empty.left.len != 0 or empty.right.len != 0) return error.EmptyChanged;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-shift: STEREO_SHIFT — the grid nudge carried into stereo, both channels in lockstep. Slide both channels by the same signed by, reusing ALES108's mono shift " ++
        "per channel; a Lotus master is a StereoClip (ALES10) whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length, so nudging a take on the grid slides both speakers by " ++
        "the same amount at once. It is the plainest lift of the family — mono shift is total (it names no out-of-range span, so it raises no fault), so this stereo twin needs no " ++
        "validation and can never refuse, and the length-preserving reseat beside stereo_silence_span (which overwrites in place) and stereo_move (which relocates). The crux is four laws. " ++
        "THE STEREO SHIFT LAW: each channel equals ALES108's mono shift on that channel byte for byte in both directions, the surviving samples sliding by by and the vacated end silent. " ++
        "THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW: left.len == right.len after the edit and both hold their starting length, the stereo image staying aligned. THE COMPOSITION-LOSS LAW, HONEST: " ++
        "stereo_shift(k) then stereo_shift(-k) returns the leading k as silence on both channels, not their bytes — shift is not invertible, the dropped edge gone; only a pre-silent " ++
        "region round-trips. THE MAGNITUDE / DEGENERATE LAW: each channel's peak re-measured through ALES13 is non-increasing, by 0 the identity on both, |by| >= len all silence on both, " ++
        "the empty stereo pair its own shift. Purely local — two bounded i16 Clips reseated through the proven mono shift, reading no byte past either channel's len; no real sample rate, " ++
        "no network, no keys, no funds, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
